  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "TRIAL EDITION - SQL longer than 10,000 characters is rejected (parse() returns -1). Full edition: https://www.sqlparser.com\n\nRelease 4.1.0.9\n\n- [DB2] Accept a cursor declared for a prepared statement name, e.g.\n  DECLARE c1 CURSOR WITH RETURN TO CALLER FOR SQL_STATEMENT, used\n  together with PREPARE. All combinations of WITH HOLD and\n  WITH RETURN [TO CALLER | TO CLIENT] are supported. Previously only\n  a cursor declared directly over a SELECT statement was accepted.\n  The prepared statement name is available on the AST through the new\n  TDb2DeclareCursorStatement.CursorStatement property.\n- [DB2] Accept the full set of declarations in the body of a compiled\n  CREATE TRIGGER, i.e. handler declarations\n  (DECLARE CONTINUE | EXIT | UNDO HANDLER FOR ...), SQLSTATE / SQLCODE\n  declarations, statement declarations and cursor declarations.\n  Previously a trigger body only accepted variable and condition\n  declarations, so a trigger with a CONTINUE HANDLER failed to parse.\n\nDemos: https://github.com/sqlparser/gsp_demo_dotnet",
